      takeaway: "At-will = either side can end it anytime, with or without cause. Term (contract) employment = a contract for a stated period; firing early without cause can be wrongful discharge.",
      rule: "<p>Employment law is built from three older areas (slide 2):</p><ul><li><b>Contract law:</b> implied contracts from promises made while hiring or in employee handbooks, promissory estoppel, and the covenant of good faith and fair dealing.</li><li><b>Agency law:</b> hiring someone to do your work (a later chapter).</li><li><b>Tort law:</b> for example, defamation over a bad reference. Employers have a <b>qualified privilege</b>: they're liable only for statements they know are false or that are mainly motivated by ill will.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Term (contract) employment</th><th>At-will employment</th></tr><tr><td>Employment contract for a stated period of time. Fired before the term ends without cause, the employer may be liable for <b>wrongful discharge</b>.</td><td>No term contract. Employee can be fired, or can quit, at any time without cause, subject to the exceptions in the next concept.</td></tr></table><p><b>Practical tip (slide 4):</b> put an at-will clause in the employee manual so the handbook can't be read as an implied contract. The sample says employment is at will, terms can change at the company's discretion, and only the CEO, in writing, can create anything other than at-will employment.</p>",
