      takeaway: "Three exceptions: labor union, public policy, and statute. Public policy has four categories: refusing to break the law, reporting a violation, acting in the public interest, and exercising a statutory right.",
      rule: "<p><b>Three exceptions to at-will firing (slide 5):</b></p><ul><li><b>Labor union exception:</b> statutes and collective bargaining agreements (CBAs) restrict firing union members.</li><li><b>Public policy exception:</b> you can't fire someone for something public policy protects (four categories below).</li><li><b>Statutory exception:</b> you can't fire in violation of federal or state statutes, such as the anti-discrimination laws in Ch. 30.</li></ul><table><tr><th>Public policy category (slide 6)</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>1. Refusing to do something state law prohibits</td><td>Refusing the employer's request to commit perjury</td></tr><tr><td>2. Reporting a violation of the law</td><td>Reporting fraudulent accounting or child labor</td></tr><tr><td>3. Acting in the public interest</td><td>Joining the National Guard, serving on a jury</td></tr><tr><td>4. Exercising a statutory right</td><td>Filing a workers' compensation claim</td></tr></table><p><b>Off-duty conduct (slide 7):</b> without a specific \"lifestyle law,\" employers can fire for off-duty conduct. Smoking is protected in only about 40% of states. Private employers may test for illegal drugs, but the EEOC bars testing for prescription drugs unless the worker seems impaired.</p><p><b>Computer monitoring (slide 8):</b> the Stored Communications Act bars unauthorized access to employees' electronic communications, but the employer may monitor if (1) the employee consents, (2) it's in the ordinary course of business, or (3) the employer provides the system.</p><p><b>Whistleblowers (slide 10)</b> are protected by the False Claims Act, SOX, Dodd-Frank, constitutional protection for government employees, and other statutes.</p><p><b>Polygraphs (slide 11):</b> under the Employee Polygraph Protection Act, employers can't require one, except for (1) an employee in an ongoing investigation, (2) government job applicants, and (3) applicants in public transport, security, banking, or pharmaceutical firms handling controlled substances.</p>",
