      rule: "<p><b>Occupational Safety and Health Act (1970), slide 12.</b> Employers must:</p><ul><li>Keep the workplace free from recognized hazards that are causing or likely to cause death or serious physical harm.</li><li>Keep records of all workplace injuries and accidents.</li><li>Post notices informing employees of their rights.</li></ul><p>The Act created the Occupational Safety and Health <b>Administration</b> (also called OSHA).</p><p><b>Workers' compensation (slides 17-18):</b></p><ul><li>Pays employees, and their families, when employees are injured in connection with their jobs.</li><li>Covers physical injuries, employment-related mental illness, and stress.</li><li>States require employers to either buy workers' comp insurance or self-insure by paying into a fund.</li><li>It is an <b>exclusive remedy</b>: workers can't sue their employer in court for damages, <b>except when the employer intentionally injures the employee</b>.</li><li>It does <b>not</b> bar suing responsible <b>third parties</b>.</li></ul>",
