      takeaway: "Under the FLSA (1938), NON-EXEMPT workers get minimum wage plus 1.5x overtime for hours over 40 in a week. EXEMPT workers (executives, administrative staff, learned professionals, the highly paid, computer workers, outside sales, teachers) get neither. The FLSA also bans oppressive child labor.",
      rule: "<p><b>Fair Labor Standards Act (1938)</b> set minimum wage and overtime requirements.</p><table><tr><th>Non-exempt (\"blue collar\")</th><th>Exempt</th></tr><tr><td>Must get the federal minimum wage for all hours worked. Overtime is <b>1.5x the regular hourly rate</b> for every hour over <b>40 in a week</b>. Students and apprentices can be paid less than the minimum wage.</td><td>No minimum wage or overtime. Includes executives, administrative employees, learned professionals, highly compensated employees, computer employees, outside sales reps, and teachers.</td></tr></table><p><b>Child labor (slides 15-16):</b> the FLSA forbids oppressive child labor and shipping goods made by businesses that use it.</p><table><tr><th>Age</th><th>Allowed work</th></tr><tr><td>Under 14</td><td>Newspaper delivery</td></tr><tr><td>14-15</td><td>Non-hazardous jobs, limited hours</td></tr><tr><td>16-17</td><td>Non-hazardous jobs, unlimited hours</td></tr><tr><td>18+</td><td>Any job</td></tr></table>",
