      takeaway: "ACA: employers with 50+ workers must offer health insurance. COBRA: keep group coverage after the job ends. Unemployment: not if you quit voluntarily or were fired for cause. ERISA: protects private pensions, and your own contributions vest immediately. WARN: 100+ employees, 60 days' notice before plant closings or mass layoffs.",
      rule: "<table><tr><th>Law</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td>Affordable Care Act (slide 20)</td><td>Employers with 50 or more workers must provide basic health insurance.</td></tr><tr><td>COBRA (slide 20)</td><td>Employees and beneficiaries can continue group health insurance after employment ends. The employer must notify them of these rights.</td></tr><tr><td>Social Security (slide 21)</td><td>Retirement, survivors', disability, and Medicare benefits. Employer and employee make matching contributions, and the self-employed pay the full amount. Failing to pay can mean interest, penalties, and criminal liability.</td></tr><tr><td>Unemployment compensation (slides 22-23)</td><td>Created under the Federal Unemployment Tax Act, run by the states, and paid for by employer unemployment taxes. Each state sets eligibility, amount, and duration. To collect you must be able to work, available for work, and seeking work, and you must not have been let go for bad conduct or have quit without just cause.</td></tr><tr><td>ERISA (slide 24)</td><td>Prevents fraud and abuse in private pension funds through record-keeping and disclosure rules. <b>Vesting</b> = a nonforfeitable right to pension benefits. Each employee's own contributions vest immediately.</td></tr><tr><td>WARN Act (slide 46)</td><td>Employers with <b>100 or more</b> employees must give <b>60 days' notice</b> before certain plant closings or layoffs.</td></tr></table>",
