      takeaway: "The EEOC enforces most federal anti-discrimination laws. You must file with the EEOC within 180 days and CAN'T sue right away. The EEOC investigates and tries mediation or conciliation. If it doesn't sue, you get a right-to-sue letter.",
      rule: "<p><b>EEOC (slide 10):</b> the federal administrative agency that enforces most federal anti-discrimination laws. Its members are appointed by the President. It can:</p><ul><li>Conduct investigations</li><li>Interpret statutes</li><li>Encourage resolution between employees and employers</li><li>Bring civil lawsuits</li><li>Seek injunctive relief</li></ul><p><b>Complaint process (slide 11):</b></p><ul><li>The employee must first file a complaint with the EEOC <b>within 180 days</b> of the discriminatory act.</li><li>The employee <b>cannot immediately sue</b> the employer.</li><li>The EEOC investigates and tries to resolve the claim, sometimes through mediation.</li><li>If the EEOC finds no violation, or finds one but decides not to sue, it issues a <b>right-to-sue letter</b>.</li></ul><p><b>Charge flow (slide 12):</b> a charge goes to mediation, further investigation, or dismissal. If a violation is found, the EEOC tries <b>conciliation</b>. If that fails, the EEOC either files suit or issues a notice of right to sue.</p>",
