      rule: "<p><b>Harassment (slide 25):</b> conduct by supervisors or coworkers that's offensive because it's sexually, racially, ethnically, or religiously charged. The target can be an applicant or an employee. Courts ask whether it creates a <b>hostile work environment</b> that unreasonably interferes with work, or is just a stray remark.</p><p><b>Sexual harassment (slide 26):</b> unwelcome sexual advances, requests for sexual favors, other harassment of a sexual nature, and offensive remarks about a person's sex, including about women in general. <b>Quid pro quo:</b> sexual favors requested in exchange for a job, promotion, or other benefit. The victim and harasser can be any sex, including the same sex.</p><table><tr><th>Who harassed</th><th>Employer liability (slides 27-28)</th></tr><tr><td>Coworker</td><td>Liable if it was <b>negligent</b> in controlling the working situation</td></tr><tr><td>Supervisor, with a tangible employment action</td><td><b>Strictly liable</b>: no defense</td></tr><tr><td>Supervisor, no tangible employment action</td><td><b>Vicariously liable unless</b> it proves an affirmative defense. The court looks at whether there's an anti-harassment policy, whether there's a complaint mechanism, whether employees were told about both, and other relevant factors.</td></tr></table>",
