      rule: "<table><tr><th>Statute</th><th>Key rule</th></tr><tr><td><b>Americans with Disabilities Act</b> (slide 36)</td><td>Employers with <b>15+</b> employees, plus public transportation, telecom, and public accommodation providers. Must <b>reasonably accommodate</b> a disabled person who can still perform the basic functions of the job, unless it's an <b>undue hardship</b> (a substantial burden in difficulty or cost). <b>Disability</b> = a physical or mental impairment that significantly limits a major life activity (walking, seeing, hearing, learning, working). The employer <b>can't ask about the disability</b> but may ask about ability to perform job functions.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Age Discrimination in Employment Act</b> (slide 35)</td><td>Employers with <b>20+</b> employees. Protects workers <b>40 and older</b> in all employment decisions, including benefits (through the Older Workers Benefit Protection Act). Allows age limits where a BFOQ exists: firefighters, police, pilots.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Equal Pay Act</b> (slide 34)</td><td>No pay disparity for jobs needing equal skill, effort, and responsibility under similar conditions. Defenses: seniority, merit, quantity or quality of production, or any factor other than sex.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Civil Rights Act of 1866, Section 1981</b> (slide 33)</td><td>All persons have the same right to make and enforce contracts \"as is enjoyed by white persons.\" Why plaintiffs use it: <b>no Title VII procedural requirements</b> (no EEOC filing) and <b>no cap</b> on compensatory or punitive damages.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act</b> (slide 37)</td><td>No discrimination based on genetic information about a person's propensity for disease.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Retaliation</b> (slide 38)</td><td>No retaliating (firing, demoting, harassing) for filing a discrimination charge or taking part in a proceeding.</td></tr><tr><td><b>USERRA</b> (1994, slide 39)</td><td>No discrimination for military service or obligations. Must rehire deployed service members, including those with service-connected disabilities that can reasonably be accommodated.</td></tr></table><p><b>Hiring pitfalls (slide 40):</b> don't ask \"How many days were you sick last year?\", \"When did you graduate from college?\" (age), \"Are you thinking of starting a family?\", or \"Where do you go to church?\" Social media checks can also turn up information that would be illegal to act on.</p>",
